    "text": "{\n  \"anomaly_detected\": true,\n  \"confidence\": 0.4,\n  \"anomalies\": [\n    {\n      \"category\": \"extra_component\",\n      \"components\": [\"U2\", \"U3\"],\n      \"pins\": [\"U2.1\", \"U3.1\", \"U2.2\", \"U3.2\", \"U2.3\", \"U3.3\", \"U2.5\", \"U3.5\", \"U2.6\", \"U3.6\", \"U2.7\", \"U3.7\", \"U2.8\", \"U3.8\"],\n      \"nets\": [\"RST\", \"POT\", \"TACH\", \"SDA\", \"PWM\", \"SCK\", \"+5V\"],\n      \"observed\": \"Two different MCUs, U2 (ATtiny45-20P) and U3 (ATtiny13V-10S), are both connected to the same nets on every signal pin: RST, POT, TACH, SDA, PWM, SCK and +5V. Both have their power pins on +5V and their GND pins on GND. The netlist carries no do-not-populate marking for either part.\",\n      \"expected\": \"A board with one fan controller MCU should have only one device driving PWM, TACH, POT and the other signals. If both parts are populated, their outputs (for example PWM at U2.6 and U3.6) fight each other. Alternate footprints for the same function should be separate, mutually exclusive placements, marked as DNP or kept on separate nets.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"PWM : J4.1(MISO,passive) R2.1(passive) R5.2(passive) U2.6(PB1,bidirectional) U3.6(PB1,bidirectional)\"},\n        {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"TACH : D1.1(K,passive) R4.1(passive) R6.2(passive) U2.3(XTAL2/PB4,bidirectional) U3.3(PB4,bidirectional)\"},\n        {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"+5V : C2.1(passive) J3.1(Pin_1,passive) J4.2(VCC,passive) J6.2(Pin_2,passive) R3.1(passive) U1.2(VO,power_out) U2.8(VCC,power_in) U3.8(VCC,power_in)\"}\n      ],\n      \"impact\": \"If both MCUs are fitted, the PWM, TACH, POT and reset lines are driven by two devices at once. This causes bus contention, unreliable fan control and possible damage to the outputs. If only one is intended, the second device is a stray part.\",\n      \"confidence\": 0.4\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"The passive values, transistor pinouts, regulator and MCU pin mappings all match standard practice and are consistent with the SPICE deck. The one concrete concern is that two different MCUs (U2 and U3) share every signal and power net with no DNP marking, which would cause output contention if both were fitted. The specification does not describe this board, so the verdict rests on general design practice and is moderately confident.\"\n}",
